# Two small computations: a straight-line fit and a Monte Carlo estimate of pi

## 1. Straight-line fit to 40 noisy points

Data: x = 0, 1, ..., 39; y = 2.5 + 0.8 x + e, with e ~ N(0, 3^2) drawn as `rng.normal(0, 3, size=40)` after `numpy.random.default_rng(227)`. Ordinary least squares; 95% confidence intervals are t-intervals with df = 38 (t = 2.0244).

| parameter | estimate | std. error | 95% CI | generating value |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| slope | 0.7484 | 0.0387 | [0.6701, 0.8267] | 0.8 |
| intercept | 3.9447 | 0.8769 | [2.1696, 5.7198] | 2.5 |

R^2 = 0.908, residual SD = 2.825 (generating SD 3). Both CIs contain the generating values.

![Data, OLS fit, 95% confidence band of the fitted line and generating line](figures/fit.png)

*Figure 1. The 40 simulated points, the OLS line, its 95% confidence band, and the generating line.*

## 2. Monte Carlo estimate of pi (`simulate.py`)

`code/simulate.py` (the unmodified script supplied in the initial state) was run once in full as `python3 -u simulate.py --out results.json`: 90 chunks x 60,000,000 points, chunk i seeded `random.Random(227+i)`, wall time 750.5 s. The estimate is the mean of the 90 chunk estimates; the interval it prints is mean +/- 1.96 * sd / sqrt(90) (normal quantile).

| quantity | value |
|---|---|
| estimate of pi | 3.1415628 |
| printed 95% interval | [3.1415193, 3.1416063] |
| pi = 3.14159265... inside the interval? | **yes** |

The full printed log is `results/simulate.log`; the numbers are in `results/results.json`. `results/simulate_partial_aborted.log` is an abandoned earlier attempt (67 of 90 chunks) and is not used anywhere in this report.

